3.65.m:
When that is established, he attains success; otherwise, the reverse. / Without having cleared away an accusation, one may not counter-charge him;
yet one may bring a counter-charge in a brawl or in acts of violence. / For both, sureties capable of determining the case should be taken.
In violence, theft, defamation, assault, and in the case of a woman, / he should conduct the litigation immediately; elsewhere, time is declared to be at discretion.
He shifts from place to place and repeatedly licks the corners of his mouth; / his forehead perspires, and his face loses its colour;
his mouth drying up and his speech faltering, he talks much that is contradictory: / whether in an accusation or in giving testimony, such a man is declared corrupt.
By appearances, gestures, gait, bodily actions, and speech, / and by the alterations of the eyes and face, the inner mind is grasped.
One who does not give a reply is to be known as defeated after three fortnights. / In all disputes, the later transaction is stronger;
but in the case of a pledge, an acceptance of gifts, or a purchase, the earlier one is stronger. / Against one who causes all trouble, when proved guilty in one part,

3.66.m:
Thus ends the three hundred and twenty-eighth chapter, entitled "Ascertainment of the Divine Ordeal," in the Haṃsagītā addressed to the sages, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra, in the third book of the glorious Viṣṇudharmottara.
3.329
Haṃsa said:
One, two, and likewise three, and four in order— / these are declared by the wise to be the wives of śūdras, vaiśyas, kṣatriyas, and brahmins.
Only a śūdra woman is remembered as the wife of a śūdra; that [śūdra woman] and his own are for a vaiśya; / those [two] and his own are for a king; and those [three] and his own are for the first-born.
A woman of a different caste should never be the [chief] wife of anyone; / only a woman of the same caste should be the senior wife, even when women of other castes are present.
Eight marriages are taught; I shall speak of their characteristics. / The Brāhma is [the giving of a maiden] after summoning; the Daiva is said to be [giving her] to officiating priests;
the Ārṣa is from receiving a pair of cattle; the Prājāpatya is likewise when requested; / the Āsura is from accepting wealth; the Gāndharva is by mutual consent of the two;
the Rākṣasa is by abduction in battle; the Paiśāca is by tricking the maiden. / The Prājāpatya, the Daiva, and the Brāhma—these three are auspicious;

the king too must cause the funerary ball to be offered for him through his servants according to rule.
Whosoever among men here is the recipient of the wealth is likewise declared to be the recipient of the debt;
and after always using what remains after the debt, O foremost of the twice-born, a man would ever be righteous.

3.67.m:
At the end of the night, the herdsman should lead the cattle to the forest;
satisfied and having drunk water, he should deliver them all in the evening.
By day, responsibility lies with the herdsman; at night, with the owner in his house.
In the event of a mishap to the cattle, the cowherd should exert himself there according to his capacity.
Even if they fall when he is unable [to save them], he should report that to the owner;
if unable to fight [the danger], he should report it to the owner, raising a cry of distress.
The cowherd, however, must bear punishment before the king
for what is lost, ruined by worms, killed by dogs, or died in an accident,
if lacking in human effort—the herdsman must deliver [its remains].
When goats and sheep are trapped by wolves, and the herdsman does not come,
whatever [beast] a wolf kills by force, that guilt falls upon the herdsman;
if, overtaken by fate while grazing together in the forest,
a wolf pounces upon and kills one, the herdsman bears no guilt in that case.
What is stolen by thieves after being fought off, the herdsman ought not to make good,
if he announces it to the owner at the proper place and time.
By this, the dispute concerning all herdsmen has been declared.

3.68.m:
