or he may eat at the fourth mealtime, or indeed at the sixth mealtime;
or he may pass the time through penances, and maintain celibacy.
Passing his time in the forest in this way, the twice-born becomes an inhabitant of the hermitage of Brahmā.
Relinquishing the bodily elements in the forest, he attains the worlds of the Great-grandfather;
and he purifies seven past generations and seven future ones, O brahmins.
3.340
Haṃsa said:
One should dwell during the fourth, final portion of one's life in the order of renouncers,
after duly performing an offering endowed with all one's possessions as sacrificial fee.
Having deposited the fires within himself, the twice-born should enter the holy order;
he should stay one night in a village and five nights in a city.
When the smoke has cleared, the pestle is set down, the embers are extinguished, and people have finished eating,
when the movement of vessels has ceased, the renouncer should continually go about for alms.
He should beg alms from seven houses, and not beg food after another beggar;
he should not be dismayed at obtaining nothing, subsisting on whatever is obtained.

The mendicant should not relish flavors, nor should he eat in anyone's house;
his vessels should be earthen, of gourd, of wood, or of split bamboo;
their purification is declared to be through scrubbing and through water.
He should take as clothing to cover his loincloth what is cast away on the road.
His dwelling should be an empty house; where evening overtakes the sage, there he is a householder;
he should apply restraint of action, restraint of mind, and restraint of speech.
He should place his foot purified by sight; he should drink water purified by cloth;
he should speak words purified by truth; he should perform action purified by the mind.
If one [man] anoints his arm with sandalwood, or if another hacks it,
he should not think of either good or ill concerning either of them.
Through breath-controls he should burn away defects; through concentrations, sin;
through sensory withdrawal, attachments; through meditation, qualities devoid of the Lord.
Free from desires, paying homage to none, viewing a clod of earth, a stone, and gold as equal,
impartial toward all beings, friendly, he attains the supreme state.
